// Heap watch: what the program can say to the ledger about its own objects
// (docs/heap-watch-design.md).
//
// API
// ---
//   heapwatch.forever(x)               -> x, marked as living as long as it likes
//   heapwatch.epoch(name, body)        -> void, runs body.run() as a unit of work
//   heapwatch.epoch_of(name, body)     -> body.make(), run as a unit of work
//   heapwatch.Work                     interface { void run(); }
//   heapwatch.Make<T>                  interface { T make(); }
//
// In a heap watch build (`./build.sh prog.m31 --heapwatch`, or -DRT_HEAPWATCH
// on both cc lines) the objects a program makes are sampled and counted, by
// allocation site, in a ledger the runtime keeps in a file; `m31c -heapwatch
// <ledger|pid>` reads it, from a running process or from one that crashed. A
// site whose oldest live object is much older than its objects normally live
// is reported as a suspect. In any other build there is no ledger, and the
// calls below do only what they do for the program: `forever` returns its
// argument and `epoch` runs its body.
//
// **`forever` is for an object that lives long on purpose**: a game's
// textures, a cache filled at startup, a connection pool. It exempts that one
// object from the age report -- it is still counted, still listed, as
// "forever", and still checked by trace -- and it changes nothing else: the
// object is freed when its last reference goes, exactly as before. A whole
// type or site is marked the same way without a rebuild, by
// `M31_AGE=Type:forever` or `M31_AGE=Type@file.m31:40:forever`.
//
// **An epoch is a unit of work that should leave nothing behind**: a
// request, a frame, a level. With epoch close on (M31_HEAPWATCH=full, or a
// test build), everything made while `body` runs is counted against the
// epoch, and the moment it ends whatever is still counted -- held only by
// other objects of the same epoch, which is a leaked cycle -- is reported in
// the ledger and the suspect log, with who holds it for a type trace is on
// for. In a test build (the corpus's, `-DRC_DEBUG`, or
// `M31_HEAPWATCH_TRAP=1`) it traps. An object leaves the epoch legitimately
// by being stored into something made before the epoch began (a captured
// list, a field of an outer object), by being sent to another thread, or by
// being the value `epoch_of` returns; every spawned thread is an epoch of its
// own too, without being asked. Epochs nest.
//
// The body is a callback (`() => handle(request)`), so it has no way to
// assign the caller's locals: what it hands back, it returns or stores.

/// Work an epoch runs and that hands nothing back.
pub interface Work {
    void run();
}

/// Work an epoch runs that hands back one value.
pub interface Make<T> {
    T make();
}

/// `value` itself, marked as living as long as it likes on purpose.
///
/// A value that is not an object -- an int, a float, a bool, a value enum --
/// is returned unchanged; there is nothing to mark.
pub T forever<T>(T value) {
    __forever(value);
    return value;
}

/// Run `body` as the unit of work `name`, and report anything it made and
/// left behind when it returns.
pub void epoch(str name, Work body) {
    // The runtime's two halves of an epoch (runtime/heapwatch.c), builtins
    // only the standard library can name: open one named `name` inside the
    // current thread, and close the innermost, reporting what it left.
    __epoch_enter(name);
    body.run();
    __epoch_leave();
}

/// Run `body` as the unit of work `name` and return what it made: that value,
/// and everything it reaches, leaves the epoch with it; anything else the
/// body made and left behind is reported when it returns.
pub T epoch_of<T>(str name, Make<T> body) {
    // Made before the epoch opens, so storing the result in it is what moves
    // the result out of the epoch -- the same rule as any store into an
    // older object, and the only way out a body has besides a send.
    List<T> out = [];
    __epoch_enter(name);
    out.push(body.make());
    __epoch_leave();
    return out.pop();
}
